---
import ProjectLayout from "../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro";
---

<ProjectLayout
  title="bee-safe-cv"
  label="RUN-EU Short Advanced Programme"
  description="On-device computer vision for detecting Varroa mites on honeybees."
  intro="A mobile app that detects Varroa mites and bee gender from photos, running the model on the phone itself. I built it with an international student team as part of a RUN-EU Short Advanced Programme on AI for beekeeping."
  stack={["Python", "YOLOv8", "ONNX", "TensorFlow Lite", "Flutter", "Roboflow"]}
>
  <section>
    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p>
      Varroa mites are one of the biggest threats to honeybee colonies, and
      beekeepers usually spot them by eye. We wanted something a beekeeper
      could point a phone at in the field, without needing a signal or a
      cloud service.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How it works</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Data.</strong> We labelled bee images in Roboflow for mites
        and bee gender.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Training.</strong> We trained a YOLOv8 object detection model
        on the labelled set.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Conversion.</strong> We exported the model to ONNX, then
        converted it to TensorFlow Lite so it was small enough for a phone.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>App.</strong> The Flutter app runs the TFLite model on the
        device and shows detections over the camera image.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How we worked</h2>
    <p>
      The team ran Scrum with Trello, used Git on Bitbucket, and held regular
      stand-ups and demos. We presented the final app to faculty and industry
      partners at Howest University of Applied Sciences in Kortrijk, Belgium.
    </p>
  </section>
</ProjectLayout>
